# {{TITLE}}

Your standalone game project. All game files are ordinary editable source.
No framework checkout, editor extension, hosted account or LLM is required to
build/play. The shared kernels are vendored and pinned in `foundation.lock.json`.

```sh
python tools/fp_game.py doctor
python tools/fp_game.py build
python tools/fp_game.py test
python tools/fp_game.py run --smoke
python tools/fp_game.py run
```

Standard Cabal also works with this project's isolated config, including a
fresh global cache:

```sh
cabal --config-file=build.config build all --offline --builddir=.build/dist
cabal --config-file=build.config test all --offline --builddir=.build/dist --test-show-details=direct
cabal --config-file=build.config run {{SLUG}} --offline --builddir=.build/dist -- --smoke
```

Use north/south/east/west to move, `exit` at E, `look`, `save`, `load`, `new`,
and `quit`. Save files live in `data/`; title/save path are in `config/game.conf`.

Read [the development guide](docs/DEVELOPMENT.md) and `GAME-SPEC.md`, or start an
agent in this directory and invoke `$game-dev`. The local skill and versioned
canonical docs travel with the project. Preserve your game code when upgrading
the foundation; compare API/pin/save changes explicitly.

The supplied foundation/template code retains its original MIT notices in
`licenses/`. Your game additions use the license you choose; currently
**{{GAME_LICENSE}}**. No commercial quality, save migration, performance,
graphics, browser or mobile guarantee follows from the initial smoke test.

## Consistent Haskell formatting

Use the [pinned project-local formatter](docs/formatting.md) for explicit setup,
non-writing checks, owned-source formatting and external HLS integration.
Run `python tools/formatter.py plan`, then `install` when permitted;
`check` never downloads and `write` never targets vendored source.
